   - Methodology and approach

To address the problem, I designed a name-matching pipeline that filters names in stages. It first applies dictionary-driven exact matching against a curated, normalized database of pornstar names (scraped from Wikipedia and other sites) using an efficient Aho–Corasick automaton. If no match is found, a Named Entity Recognition (NER) fallback is used, leveraging both Transformer-based and SpaCy models to extract PERSON entities from high-frequency queries—balancing accuracy with computational cost.



This two-stage process ensures both high precision and recall. After NER, extracted names are verified against the curated dataset to avoid misclassifying general celebrities or unrelated personal names as pornstars.



The core approach I applied is dictionary-driven exact matching. All queries are first normalized (lowercasing, Unicode normalization, punctuation, and whitespace cleanup). This is to make sure about consistent matching across highly unstructured search input. A curated external dataset of pornstar names, scraped from sites, serves as the source of truth. These names are normalized identically and indexed using an Aho–Corasick automaton, enabling efficient multi-pattern matching across tens of thousands of names in a single pass per query. This ensures deterministic behavior and high precision, especially for full-name matches (e.g., “mia khalifa” instead of fragmented tokens).



Performance considerations are central to the design. The pipeline prioritizes high-count queries, avoids unnecessary NER inference, and supports batched processing and GPU acceleration where available. The final output preserves the original dataset structure while enriching it with a structured names column, making it suitable for downstream analytics and business intelligence workflows.



